From: minsky@media.mit.edu (Marvin Minsky)
Date: Wed, 19 Oct 94 12:25:57 -0400
Subject: [#94-10-337] URL: CSICOP/_Skeptical Inquirer_

 "Patrick M. Fitzgerald" <pmfitzge@fitz.b30.ingr.com posted this from the
"About CSICOP" page of the _Skeptical Inquirer_
>        http://iquest.com/~fitz/csicop/

>   The more serious-minded among us ask what is going
>   on. Why the sudden explosion of interest, even among some
>   otherwise sensible people, in all sorts of paranormal
>   "happenings"? Are we in retreat from the scientific ideas
>   of rationality, dispassionate examination of evidence,
>   and sober experiment that have made modern civilization
>   what it is today?
>
>   For a long time, the raising and answering of such
>   questions have been left to commentators and
>   journalists. Eighteen years ago, scientists started to
>   fight back. They set up an organization - the Committee
>   for the Scientific Investigation of Claims of the
>   Paranormal - to investigate unusual phenomena.

Well, the generic  unusual phenomenon is the rise of uncritical thinking
itself.  What has caused it to increase?  I have the feeling that it may
largely stem from

  * The rise of "tolerance" in the 1960s:
        All religions are equally valid.
        All ideas are equally good.
        All cultures have equal merit.

How did this meme grow so rapidly.  (Assuming that it actually did.)  I
suppose that what happened is that once the meta-tolerance virus got
properly "coated", each nutty idea-monger recognized that this was a way to
defuse objections to that particular ridiculosity.  The general-purpose
political-correctness meme then began to spread very rapidly.  In my
informal survey, less than a quarter of MIT students in my lecture course
were willing to state, for example, that "Astrology has no merit whatever,"
for fear (I presume) of being considered too rigid to tolerate the views of
others.

Assuming that this picture is correct -- that a malignant tolerance-meme
became epidemic in or around to 1960s -- I'd like to hear your conjectures
about details of why it became so successful.

        Was there something special about the cultural substrate?
        Did it have a peculiarly effective virus-coat?
        Was it a combination of several mind-softening factors?

CSICOP deserves applause for tracking down particular forms of fraud,
gullibility, etc.  However, it seems to me that we need to get to the root
of the problem.  We must understand the nature of the generalized
gullibility meme itself.

  -----------------------

By the way, I discovered what might be a pretty good vaccine or
immunization technique.  When my children were young I hired a magician to
give them magic lessons.    Cheap, incidentally: most magicians cannot find
work, so they'll come to your house for a very small fee.   Kids who have
already mastered the arts of deception magic are harder for others to fool.


