I am NOT a beleiver in Astrology, but I do feel the need to point out 
that the stars may very well produce some sort of effect that is not yet 
accounted for in the Einstein's gravity equations.

Newton and Einstein declared that any motion can be view from any frame 
of reference.  (Everything is relative, so to speak).  This is clearly 
NOT true in the case of rotation.  When you stir a cup of coffee, the 
surface depresses in the middle and pushes up near the edge, due to 
centrifugal force (inertia).  But, from the Coffee's frame of reference, 
it is the rest of the world that is spinning madly.  Why, then, 
does the coffee make that bowl shape?

Ernst Mach said that it was because the Coffee was rotating within an 
INERTIAL frame of reference, perhaps created by the mass of the solar 
system.  He proposed no mechanism for this.  Furthermore, he said that 
the rotation of planets and such occured within an intertial frame of 
reference generated by the "fixed stars."  Again, no equations or 
mechanisms.  If he was right, we have no idea how this would work, or how 
strong an effect it is.  If he was wrong, than physics is sort of stuck.

If anyone knows a more current answer to "Mach's Principle," let me know. 


Sorry, that's a pretty long explanation for why stars might form some 
