From: sasha1@netcom.com (Alexander Chislenko)
Date: Wed, 19 Oct 1994 13:37:40 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-346] Tolerance and nonsense.

   I agree with Marvin Minsky that tolerance contributes to the rise of
irrational thinking, and applaud his consistent, passionate and vocal
intolerance of nonsense ideas that I have often had opportunity and pleasure
to observe.



  I would attribute the rise of indiscriminating tolerance mainly to
the recent American social reconciliation efforts under conditions of
considerable (by all historical standards) diversity, augmented by rapid
increase in population mixing - due to improving transportation (postwar
highway and airport construction), richer media coverage of various locations
and subcultures, economic integration, greater workforce mobility, etc.

  Some of these conditions apply to other contemporary cultures, especially
to Europe, some don't - e.g., in culturally uniform countries increased
population mobility doesn't put social tolerance standards under pressure.

  An additional tolerance-enhancing factor that has been working recently
in practically all countries, is rapid globalization of all aspects of social
life, with increasing exposure of people to foreign cultural artefacts.

  Tolerance seems a natural result of these tendencies, and can be viewed
as a generally positive trend - it greases the wheels of the economic
globalization, decreases chances of wars and opens people up psychologically.
People get a chance to rethink some of their less significant views, allow
more freedom to their children and neighbors, and rethink what parts of their
culture are really important to them.

  However, since people usually have little ability (or interest) to
contemplate the scope and direction of the processes shaping their meme sets,
they spend a lot of time fighting over insignificant details of the process
('what hair styles should we allow in public schools of our district?') and
jump to oversimplified conclusions on the general issues.

  It does not surprise me that the first swing of public attitude to the
diversity explosion may be one of indiscriminating tolerance.  It's better
than an all-out war, anyway.  Later it may swing back in a move as misguided
as this one.


  Of course, social integration is not the only shaping factor here; in
order for the idea of tolerance to grow into the perverse, indiscriminate
acceptance of crap ideas, the cultural soil has to be pre-fertilized with
a lot of memetic manure...
  I don't have time to dig in it now, though.

 ------------------------------------------------------------------

  Still, tolerance can only *weaken the opposition* to bogus ideas.
People who are simply tolerant - no matter how much - are not the
ones who are actively promoting those ideas.

  Memetic infections are not propelled by tolerance - just facilitated.

  The key reason to increased _susceptibility_ of [small] minds to irrational
memes seems to lie in the widening gap between the growing complexity of the
social environment and the stagnantly modest ability of an average citizen to
understand what is going on. People are finding that science no longer allows
them to build a simple set of all-explaining ideas that would give them an
illusion of control and safety.  In such conditions, they are ready to take
drastic measures to simplify their meme-sets, and push the rest of the world
out of their sphere of consideration, interests, and responsibility.

  This is the tendency that propels both crap theories and totalitarianism.
"Furer thinks for us". Or the Psychic Network. Life gets so temptingly
simple... People gain their calm, confidence and dignity, they satisfy the
need to admire the Unknown without having to think how it works - and then
they can switch the channel to watch baseball.

  It's a fools' paradise!  You can't lure them out of there with any rational
theories. And why should you?  Leave them alone.  Let humans, animals and
plants stay filled with bogus models of the world. It's their nature.
Just don't let them control your life.


  I find some consolation in the thought that people who are filled with
stupid dead-end ideas, even if directed into the best fields, would hardly
produce anything truly valuable anyway.
  Yes, they contaminate traditional social decision-making structures and
make sure that the frontier development gets [still] fewer resources.
Fortunately enough though, the blanket tolerance and general stupidity that
help them mislead themselves also prevent them from intruding into the most
intimate domain of real researchers - formation and development of serious
ideas.

		-----------------------------

 One way to confront the rise of irrationality in the circles that _do_ matter
is to use the power you have to support and promote people with clear minds -
e.g., launching the author of this message into the astrology-infected MIT
milieu.   ;-)
  If some day the Great Thinkers start supporting like-minded people as
vigorously as their religious adversaries do, they may find fewer reasons
to be unhappy with their environments...


